neutron-zzzzzzzzze-ime-dont-claim-unconvertible-keys

Don't route keys through the IME on a driver that cannot convert them, and give the
IME's fallback re-post a well-formed keystroke lParam.

SYMPTOM (Adobe Premiere Pro, user-reported, reproduced and fixed 2026-08-19): pressing
Backspace ONCE in a text field deletes TWO characters. Arrow keys move one, and typing
inserts one, so it looked app-specific for months. It is not.

CAUSE. winewayland.drv activates an IME HKL (get_ime_hkl() -> 0xE001xxxx) for every
window the compositor gives text-input focus (wayland_text_input.c text_input_enter),
i.e. effectively all of them. That makes is_ime_hkl() true, so the built-in IME claims
EVERY keystroke -- imm32/ime.c ImeProcessKey returns TRUE for anything but a modifier
("TODO: should be ctx->fOpen"). win32u then rewrites the message to VK_PROCESSKEY.

But winewayland implements no pImeToAsciiEx at all, so __wine_set_user_driver fills it
with nulldrv_ImeToAsciiEx, and every conversion returns STATUS_NOT_IMPLEMENTED -- 
visible in a +imm trace as "ime_driver_call processing vkey 0x8, scan 0xe -> 3221225474"
for every single key. imm32 then re-posts the original key. The app therefore receives
TWO WM_KEYDOWNs per physical press where Windows delivers one, and its own
TranslateMessage turns the second into a WM_CHAR that the Windows message stream, for
that keystroke, does not contain.

The re-posted message was also malformed: TRANSMSG.lParam was set to the raw scan value,
putting the scan code in the REPEAT COUNT field and leaving the scan-code field zero. A
Backspace arrived as "repeat count 14, scan 0" instead of Windows' 0x000E0001, and the
WM_CHAR derived from it inherited that.

FIX.
  1. win32u: only offer a key to the IME if the driver actually implements ImeToAsciiEx
     (new user_driver_can_ime_to_ascii() in driver.c, compared against nulldrv). Claiming
     a key that can never be converted cannot do anything except duplicate it. Drivers
     that do implement IME conversion are unaffected.
  2. imm32: rebuild a well-formed lParam, MAKELONG(1, vsc), on the fallback re-post.

Composition is unaffected: winewayland still activates the IME HKL, and the compositor's
preedit/commit arrives through the async WM_WINE_IME_NOTIFY path, which never depended
on the per-key claim.

MEASURED, live Premiere, +timestamp,+key,+imm, properties panel:
                     before                          after
  messages           VK_PROCESSKEY + re-posted       VK_BACK + WM_CHAR
                     VK_BACK + WM_CHAR lParam        lParam 000e0001
                     0000000e
  caret reports      225 -> 222 -> 212 (2 edits)     222 -> 212 (1 edit)
  VK_PROCESSKEY      every keystroke                 zero in the whole session
User-confirmed: one Backspace, one character.

Causation was proven before the build by preserved-fixes/harnesses/ime-off-probe.c, which
posts WM_INPUTLANGCHANGEREQUEST(0x04090409) to take the app off the IME layout at runtime.

Full write-up: docs/wiki/TODO.md P1d.

--- a/dlls/imm32/ime.c
+++ b/dlls/imm32/ime.c
@@ -598,7 +598,13 @@
 
     if (status == STATUS_NOT_IMPLEMENTED)
     {
-        TRANSMSG msg = {.message = vsc & KF_UP ? WM_KEYUP : WM_KEYDOWN, .wParam = vkey, .lParam = vsc};
+        /* NEUTRON: rebuild a well-formed keystroke lParam. Passing the raw scan value
+         * put it in the REPEAT COUNT field and left the scan-code field zero, so the
+         * re-posted key -- and the WM_CHAR the app's TranslateMessage derives from it --
+         * looked nothing like a real keystroke (Backspace arrived as repeat count 14,
+         * scan 0). Windows sends 0x000E0001 here; so do we now. */
+        TRANSMSG msg = {.message = vsc & KF_UP ? WM_KEYUP : WM_KEYDOWN, .wParam = vkey,
+                        .lParam = MAKELONG(1, vsc)};
         msgs->TransMsg[count++] = msg;
     }
     else if (status) WARN( "WINE_IME_TO_ASCII_EX returned unexpected status %#lx\n", status );
--- a/dlls/win32u/driver.c
+++ b/dlls/win32u/driver.c
@@ -1338,6 +1338,17 @@
 /******************************************************************************
  *	     __wine_set_user_driver   (win32u.so)
  */
+/* NEUTRON: TRUE only if the display driver can actually convert a key through an IME.
+ * A driver that doesn't implement ImeToAsciiEx gets nulldrv_ImeToAsciiEx above (see
+ * SET_USER_FUNC), whose every call returns STATUS_NOT_IMPLEMENTED -- so claiming keys
+ * for such a driver can never do anything except duplicate them. winewayland.drv is
+ * exactly that case: it implements SetIMECompositionRect but no ImeToAsciiEx. */
+BOOL user_driver_can_ime_to_ascii(void)
+{
+    if (user_driver == &lazy_load_driver) return FALSE;
+    return user_driver->pImeToAsciiEx != nulldrv_ImeToAsciiEx;
+}
+
 void __wine_set_user_driver( const struct user_driver_funcs *funcs, UINT version )
 {
     struct user_driver_funcs *driver, *prev;
--- a/dlls/win32u/message.c
+++ b/dlls/win32u/message.c
@@ -2562,7 +2562,17 @@
     }
     msg->pt = point_phys_to_win_dpi( msg->hwnd, msg->pt );
 
-    if (remove && (msg->message == WM_KEYDOWN || msg->message == WM_KEYUP))
+    /* NEUTRON: only offer the key to the IME if the driver can actually convert it.
+     * winewayland activates an IME HKL (0xE001xxxx) for every window the compositor
+     * gives text-input focus, which makes the built-in IME claim EVERY keystroke --
+     * but it implements no ImeToAsciiEx, so imm32 gets STATUS_NOT_IMPLEMENTED and
+     * simply re-posts the key. The app therefore sees TWO WM_KEYDOWNs per physical
+     * press where Windows delivers one, and translates the second into a WM_CHAR the
+     * Windows message stream never contains. Measured in Premiere Pro: one Backspace
+     * deleted two characters (docs/wiki/TODO.md P1d). Claiming a key that can never
+     * be converted buys nothing, so don't. */
+    if (remove && user_driver_can_ime_to_ascii() &&
+        (msg->message == WM_KEYDOWN || msg->message == WM_KEYUP))
         if (ImmProcessKey( msg->hwnd, NtUserGetKeyboardLayout(0), msg->wParam, msg->lParam, 0 ))
             msg->wParam = VK_PROCESSKEY;
 
--- a/dlls/win32u/win32u_private.h
+++ b/dlls/win32u/win32u_private.h
@@ -357,6 +357,7 @@
 
 /* driver.c */
 extern const struct user_driver_funcs *user_driver;
+extern BOOL user_driver_can_ime_to_ascii(void);
 extern struct client_surface *nulldrv_client_surface_create( HWND hwnd );
 
 extern ULONG_PTR zero_bits;
